WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.849
- He grew up poor and was
the first in his family

00:00:02.849 --> 00:00:04.157
to go to college.

00:00:04.157 --> 00:00:07.329
He became Fortune Magazine's 2011

00:00:07.329 --> 00:00:09.206
Business Person of the Year.

00:00:09.206 --> 00:00:10.818
He's one of the richest men in America

00:00:10.818 --> 00:00:14.383
with a net worth of 1.5 billion dollars.

00:00:14.383 --> 00:00:16.157
He's Howard Schultz and here are his

00:00:16.157 --> 00:00:18.324
top ten rules for success.

00:00:21.386 --> 00:00:25.958
- I feel so strongly
that the reason I'm here

00:00:25.958 --> 00:00:27.958
is I dreamed big dreams.

00:00:29.479 --> 00:00:30.998
I dreamed the kind of dreams

00:00:30.998 --> 00:00:35.023
that other people said
would not be possible.

00:00:35.023 --> 00:00:37.418
I'm also living proof this
isn't a Hollywood movie.

00:00:37.418 --> 00:00:40.423
I come literally from the
other side of the tracks.

00:00:40.423 --> 00:00:42.464
If you saw where I grew up

00:00:42.464 --> 00:00:44.003
and we took a picture of it,

00:00:44.003 --> 00:00:46.662
the neighborhood, the setting.

00:00:46.662 --> 00:00:49.346
You say it's not, it's impossible

00:00:49.346 --> 00:00:52.974
that someone could come
from there and get here.

00:00:52.974 --> 00:00:54.649
But it is possible.

00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:58.213
And not to sound too trite
it's possible because

00:00:58.213 --> 00:01:01.707
we still live in a country
where the entrepreneurial spirit

00:01:01.707 --> 00:01:03.707
is so alive and so well.

00:01:05.819 --> 00:01:07.726
And anyone in this country

00:01:07.726 --> 00:01:11.893
if you dream big and surround
yourself with great people

00:01:12.904 --> 00:01:14.886
and leave your ego at the door

00:01:14.886 --> 00:01:17.622
and share a success with others.

00:01:17.622 --> 00:01:19.681
And have a little luck along the way

00:01:19.681 --> 00:01:21.857
you can do anything.

00:01:21.857 --> 00:01:24.700
But there will be people along the way

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.494
and I remember this, who will say to you,

00:01:27.494 --> 00:01:30.551
you know you're dreaming too big.

00:01:30.551 --> 00:01:32.153
It's not possible.

00:01:32.153 --> 00:01:35.249
Those things just can't happen.

00:01:35.249 --> 00:01:36.832
Don't allow anyone,

00:01:38.003 --> 00:01:39.253
friend, family,

00:01:40.569 --> 00:01:42.398
acquaintances, teachers

00:01:42.398 --> 00:01:46.397
whoever it is, don't
allow anyone to tell you

00:01:46.397 --> 00:01:49.948
that what you are dreaming
for yourself and your family

00:01:49.948 --> 00:01:51.857
is not possible.

00:01:51.857 --> 00:01:53.107
It is possible.

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:57.103
Don't be one of those
people 20 years from now

00:01:57.103 --> 00:01:59.847
are going to be walking
around a nine-to-five job

00:01:59.847 --> 00:02:02.987
miserable and angry and bitter

00:02:02.987 --> 00:02:07.021
because you let your
dreams get away from you.

00:02:07.021 --> 00:02:09.197
Because you did not
believe stronger enough,

00:02:09.197 --> 00:02:12.729
strongly enough in yourself
and what was possible.

00:02:12.729 --> 00:02:15.095
Don't allow that to happen.

00:02:15.095 --> 00:02:17.512
This is an unbelievable time.

00:02:19.977 --> 00:02:23.013
And I can't encourage
you enough to continue

00:02:23.013 --> 00:02:25.458
to think that things are possible

00:02:25.458 --> 00:02:27.496
and that your dreams can come true.

00:02:27.496 --> 00:02:29.670
How many people have you met in your life

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:32.766
who have said things
like, "I had that idea."

00:02:32.766 --> 00:02:34.238
"I knew about that."

00:02:34.238 --> 00:02:37.838
Or I, "I was so close but I gave up."

00:02:37.838 --> 00:02:40.888
I think sometimes the
difference between success

00:02:40.888 --> 00:02:44.813
and failure, winning and
losing, is a very fine line

00:02:44.813 --> 00:02:48.701
between those people who'll
continue to move forward.

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:53.693
In August of 87 we had 11
stores and a hundred employees

00:02:53.693 --> 00:02:57.239
and a dream to create a
national retail company

00:02:57.239 --> 00:02:59.314
around the coffee experience,

00:02:59.314 --> 00:03:00.889
good coffee in America.

00:03:00.889 --> 00:03:02.955
But there was another dream I had.

00:03:02.955 --> 00:03:06.146
And that was to build a
different type of company.

00:03:06.146 --> 00:03:09.128
A company that potentially could achieve

00:03:09.128 --> 00:03:12.291
the delicate balance between profitability

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:15.931
and the fiscal responsibility
of building shareholder value.

00:03:15.931 --> 00:03:18.904
But the balance of also having a heart,

00:03:18.904 --> 00:03:22.006
benevolence and integrating
a social conscience

00:03:22.006 --> 00:03:24.910
in everything the company would do.

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:27.980
And hopefully one day,
remember this was '87,

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:31.803
the company would be known
as much for its values,

00:03:31.803 --> 00:03:34.157
its culture, its guiding principles

00:03:34.157 --> 00:03:37.324
as it would the quality of its coffee.

00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:42.121
Fast forward from '87
to '89 and '80 and 1990

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.978
the company was beginning
to get some traction.

00:03:44.978 --> 00:03:46.741
We had about 50 stores.

00:03:46.741 --> 00:03:49.010
We still were not making money.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:50.822
And I think we all realized at the time

00:03:50.822 --> 00:03:54.977
that we were not in the
coffee business serving people

00:03:54.977 --> 00:03:58.812
but we were in the people
business serving coffee.

00:03:58.812 --> 00:04:02.557
That if we wanted to exceed the
expectations of the customer

00:04:02.557 --> 00:04:06.409
we had to first exceed the
expectations of our people.

00:04:06.409 --> 00:04:10.507
We made a very important
strategic decision early on

00:04:10.507 --> 00:04:13.448
that franchising could not maintain

00:04:13.448 --> 00:04:15.790
the culture and values of the company.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:19.611
That all it would be would be
an access to cheap capital.

00:04:19.611 --> 00:04:23.294
And so we continued on track
of opening and building

00:04:23.294 --> 00:04:25.450
a company owned system.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:27.688
I've said for years that
that you to celebrate

00:04:27.688 --> 00:04:31.079
your victories and really
celebrate your mistakes.

00:04:31.079 --> 00:04:33.647
And what I mean by that
is that you have to learn

00:04:33.647 --> 00:04:36.181
from the mistakes that you make.

00:04:36.181 --> 00:04:37.892
And you've got to create
an environment where

00:04:37.892 --> 00:04:40.605
people are not afraid to take a risk

00:04:40.605 --> 00:04:43.504
and not afraid to do
things that are beyond

00:04:43.504 --> 00:04:45.187
what the expectations are.

00:04:45.187 --> 00:04:47.828
At the same time you've
got to set up a framework

00:04:47.828 --> 00:04:50.189
for how those things
are managed and judged

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:51.899
and evaluated.

00:04:51.899 --> 00:04:54.821
But I believed very
strongly that we could build

00:04:54.821 --> 00:04:56.682
a different kind of company.

00:04:56.682 --> 00:04:59.511
I never believed, never.

00:04:59.511 --> 00:05:01.519
I think it would have
been arrogant to believe

00:05:01.519 --> 00:05:04.514
that we could have a thousand, 2,000

00:05:04.514 --> 00:05:08.399
or 7,500 stores in the
US and around the world.

00:05:08.399 --> 00:05:11.653
But something really
magical started happening

00:05:11.653 --> 00:05:15.820
and it started happening as
a result of not advertising

00:05:16.976 --> 00:05:19.536
or conventional consumer

00:05:19.536 --> 00:05:21.192
classical marketing.

00:05:21.192 --> 00:05:23.869
The fact is that Starbucks
spends very little

00:05:23.869 --> 00:05:25.289
on traditional advertising.

00:05:25.289 --> 00:05:29.368
It started happening because
we demonstrated to ourselves

00:05:29.368 --> 00:05:33.535
and now the world that to
build an enduring, sustainable,

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:38.388
long-term business and consumer brand

00:05:38.388 --> 00:05:41.691
it's not about fancy marketing.

00:05:41.691 --> 00:05:45.352
It's about establishing a
very powerful and unusual

00:05:45.352 --> 00:05:48.690
emotional relationship with the customer.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:52.771
But I'm also here to tell
you that I feel strongly

00:05:52.771 --> 00:05:55.308
that long-term value for your shareholders

00:05:55.308 --> 00:05:59.538
cannot be achieved in an
enduring sustainable way

00:05:59.538 --> 00:06:02.959
unless the management of
the company is completely

00:06:02.959 --> 00:06:05.003
and comprehensively committed to creating

00:06:05.003 --> 00:06:07.836
long-term value for the employees.

00:06:08.815 --> 00:06:11.853
And the two are directly linked together.

00:06:11.853 --> 00:06:14.686
And any company, any management team

00:06:14.686 --> 00:06:16.716
that turns its back on its people

00:06:16.716 --> 00:06:19.324
and doesn't share the
success of the company

00:06:19.324 --> 00:06:21.571
with the people who do the work

00:06:21.571 --> 00:06:23.661
are not going to finish the race.

00:06:23.661 --> 00:06:25.355
I think something that
has happened the last

00:06:25.355 --> 00:06:27.150
couple of years that I have noticed

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.518
which I think is powerful and it is a

00:06:29.518 --> 00:06:31.898
see change is that the consumer

00:06:31.898 --> 00:06:34.841
has begun in many ways to perform

00:06:34.841 --> 00:06:38.321
what I would call loosely a cultural audit

00:06:38.321 --> 00:06:40.807
of companies, of brands,

00:06:40.807 --> 00:06:43.037
and of products and services that they are

00:06:43.037 --> 00:06:45.692
asked to support, to buy.

00:06:45.692 --> 00:06:47.981
And the cultural audit
goes something like,

00:06:47.981 --> 00:06:51.231
for the first time businesses recognize

00:06:52.824 --> 00:06:55.604
that there is parity in the marketplace.

00:06:55.604 --> 00:06:57.682
Parity on price.

00:06:57.682 --> 00:07:00.230
Parity on features and benefits.

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:02.688
And the consumer, as a result of that,

00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:05.134
has significant choices as to what they're

00:07:05.134 --> 00:07:07.346
going to buy and support.

00:07:07.346 --> 00:07:08.902
But for the first time
they're asking questions

00:07:08.902 --> 00:07:12.545
beyond the characteristics of
the product, or the service.

00:07:12.545 --> 00:07:15.164
They're asking about values.

00:07:15.164 --> 00:07:18.080
They're asking questions about how you

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:19.887
take care of your people.

00:07:19.887 --> 00:07:22.850
Whether or not you're
giving back to the community

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:25.476
in which they live and work.

00:07:25.476 --> 00:07:29.643
I think that success in
business is a very difficult

00:07:30.713 --> 00:07:32.530
thing to achieve.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:35.958
And it's more difficult today
probably than any other time

00:07:35.958 --> 00:07:39.028
despite the vast number of opportunities.

00:07:39.028 --> 00:07:41.567
And as it relates to finding your calling

00:07:41.567 --> 00:07:43.389
in terms of what the opportunity

00:07:43.389 --> 00:07:46.324
or the entrepreneurial
adventure is going to be

00:07:46.324 --> 00:07:51.117
it has to be linked to something
that you are so passionate

00:07:51.117 --> 00:07:53.641
and crazed about that you're willing to

00:07:53.641 --> 00:07:55.626
knock down walls and climb mountains

00:07:55.626 --> 00:07:58.332
and do the things that
are going to put yourself

00:07:58.332 --> 00:08:01.537
in a position of sacrificing
so many other things

00:08:01.537 --> 00:08:02.639
for its success.

00:08:02.639 --> 00:08:05.559
And you can only do that
when there's a compelling

00:08:05.559 --> 00:08:07.716
underlying feeling that you have

00:08:07.716 --> 00:08:10.858
that this is something I'm
going to fight to the death for.

00:08:10.858 --> 00:08:12.993
And without that, I think
it's going to be a commodity

00:08:12.993 --> 00:08:15.178
based experience not
only for the customer,

00:08:15.178 --> 00:08:16.489
but for you.

00:08:16.489 --> 00:08:18.978
And I think that you can't really succeed

00:08:18.978 --> 00:08:20.588
at the levels that you want to

00:08:20.588 --> 00:08:22.309
unless you find that thing

00:08:22.309 --> 00:08:24.394
that you're so passionate, excited,

00:08:24.394 --> 00:08:25.703
and in love with.

00:08:25.703 --> 00:08:27.547
And I think because you've
got it along the way

00:08:27.547 --> 00:08:30.238
you're going to have to
convince so many other people

00:08:30.238 --> 00:08:33.740
to believe in you and the
idea and the opportunity

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:36.311
and the struggle and the obstacles that

00:08:36.311 --> 00:08:39.055
are going to come can over only be

00:08:39.055 --> 00:08:42.123
overcome by this passion that you have

00:08:42.123 --> 00:08:44.013
in which you're going to be able to get

00:08:44.013 --> 00:08:47.009
people to follow you over those hurdles.

00:08:47.009 --> 00:08:50.449
I know it sounds a little bit
sophomoric but it's so true.

00:08:50.449 --> 00:08:54.616
This is the key of unlocking
the success of our business

00:08:55.964 --> 00:08:58.177
and hopefully the
businesses that you either

00:08:58.177 --> 00:09:02.421
are thinking about,
working for, or a part of.

00:09:02.421 --> 00:09:04.838
And it's this, it's so basic.

00:09:08.231 --> 00:09:10.481
We all want the same thing.

00:09:13.606 --> 00:09:16.420
Break break away the differences between

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:19.920
what I just described, language, politics.

00:09:20.786 --> 00:09:23.164
The way people dress, their history.

00:09:23.164 --> 00:09:24.301
It doesn't matter.

00:09:24.301 --> 00:09:26.138
We all want the same thing.

00:09:26.138 --> 00:09:28.055
We want to be respected

00:09:29.085 --> 00:09:30.168
as employees.

00:09:31.287 --> 00:09:34.537
We want to be appreciated as customers.

00:09:35.735 --> 00:09:39.670
We want to feel dignified in the workplace

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:41.910
and who we are as people.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.410
And we want to go into a store

00:09:45.768 --> 00:09:47.450
and for that brief moment of whether

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:51.754
we're buying a cup of coffee
or whatever it might be,

00:09:51.754 --> 00:09:54.504
we want some value for our money.

00:09:57.367 --> 00:10:00.534
And the human condition of all of that

00:10:01.691 --> 00:10:04.396
between business and the
consumer and everything

00:10:04.396 --> 00:10:07.228
everything everything else in between

00:10:07.228 --> 00:10:10.811
is the human condition
of the human spirit.

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:15.907
If you can exceed the
expectations of your people,

00:10:17.721 --> 00:10:20.127
if they feel they're
working in an enterprise

00:10:20.127 --> 00:10:24.142
or a company that values
them as human beings

00:10:24.142 --> 00:10:27.085
and not as paychecks values.

00:10:27.085 --> 00:10:29.752
Values them as human
beings and not a line-item

00:10:29.752 --> 00:10:31.502
on the balance sheet.

00:10:33.393 --> 00:10:37.922
And they in turn can take that
feeling of being respected

00:10:37.922 --> 00:10:40.129
and appreciated as an employee

00:10:40.129 --> 00:10:42.728
and share that with someone else.

00:10:42.728 --> 00:10:44.516
A paying customer.

00:10:44.516 --> 00:10:47.416
Whether it's in Kuwait or Dubai

00:10:47.416 --> 00:10:49.301
or the opening of Starbucks France

00:10:49.301 --> 00:10:51.692
where people said it wouldn't be possible.

00:10:51.692 --> 00:10:54.609
It can work, and it is sustainable.

00:10:56.109 --> 00:10:59.526
Because the human spirit has no language.

00:11:00.838 --> 00:11:04.056
And in a time in the world when there is

00:11:04.056 --> 00:11:07.357
such cynicism and a fracturing of trust

00:11:07.357 --> 00:11:08.690
if you reach out

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:13.489
and appreciate and respect
another human being.

00:11:13.489 --> 00:11:15.489
it comes back to you you

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:29.181
Because not every decision
is an economic decision.

00:11:29.181 --> 00:11:32.564
Despite the fact that the site statistics

00:11:32.564 --> 00:11:34.719
that are narrowing crime

00:11:34.719 --> 00:11:38.329
we did provide a 38% shareholder return

00:11:38.329 --> 00:11:39.457
over last year.

00:11:39.457 --> 00:11:41.735
I don't know how many (mumbling) invested

00:11:41.735 --> 00:11:44.797
but I would suspect not many
things, companies, products,

00:11:44.797 --> 00:11:47.220
investments, have been termed 38% over

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:48.132
the last couple of months.

00:11:48.132 --> 00:11:51.048
Having said that, it is
not an economic decision.

00:11:51.048 --> 00:11:53.506
The lens in which we
are making that decision

00:11:53.506 --> 00:11:55.938
is through the lens of our people.

00:11:55.938 --> 00:11:59.029
We employ over 200,000
people in this company

00:11:59.029 --> 00:12:01.696
and we want to embrace diversity

00:12:02.821 --> 00:12:05.071
(applause)

00:12:19.051 --> 00:12:20.768
If you think respectively,
that you can get a higher

00:12:20.768 --> 00:12:23.254
return than 38% you got last year

00:12:23.254 --> 00:12:24.868
it's a free country.

00:12:24.868 --> 00:12:27.370
You can sell your shares of Starbucks

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:28.795
and buy shares in other
companies, thank you.

00:12:28.795 --> 00:12:31.045
(applause)

00:12:32.069 --> 00:12:33.251
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:12:33.251 --> 00:12:36.533
I made this video because
Catan JIJ 95 asked me to.

00:12:36.533 --> 00:12:38.108
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want

00:12:38.108 --> 00:12:39.858
me to profile leave it
in the comments below

00:12:39.858 --> 00:12:41.310
and I'll see what I can do.

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:43.067
And I'd also love to know which of the

00:12:43.067 --> 00:12:45.704
top 10 lessons from
Howard you love the best.

00:12:45.704 --> 00:12:47.586
Leave it below, I'm going
to joining the discussion

00:12:47.586 --> 00:12:49.419
and I'll see you soon.

